ative substructures to create node-wise views with a GNN-based rationale finder. However, the single-view (i.e., only node- or edge-wise) confines the model\u2019s performance to a sub-optimal position, which is consistent with the observations in (Chen et al., 2020; You et al., 2020). To improve the diversity of views, a naive solution is to adopt multiple auxiliary finders, each of which specialises one view. Nonetheless, you cannot have your cake and eat it \u2013 by this way, more diversity on rationale-aware transformations demands more complexity on systems (i.e., more auxiliary finders).\nRationale Encoder. Apart from the rationale finder, existing rationale-aware GCL frameworks separate the rationale finder from the encoder (i.e., the backbone model). The back-propagation of gradients to the rationale encoder is natural when optimizing the contrastive loss of representations yielded by it. Nevertheless, to enable the propagation to the rationale finder, AD-GCL Suresh et al. (2021) leverages the Gumbel-Max reparameterization, which may lead to instability stemming from a large variance of gradients (Jang et al., 2017). And RGCL (Li et al., 2022) mixes the outputs of rationale encoder and finder to generate graph representations to contrast, making the output of the encoder biased \u2013 the rationale finder will be discarded after pre-training. Here, we blame the separation of rationale finder and encoder for the aforementioned instability and biased representations."
